/*!
  \page install_macos Building on macOS (community-supported)
  macOS is not one of the \ref install_supportedos "officially supported operating systems" for PandA/Bambu.
  It is possible to build the framework natively on macOS (as opposed to using the \ref install_docker "Docker image"),
  but doing so currently requires one important workaround described below. This page assumes you are otherwise
  following the generic \ref install_source "build from source" instructions, using a Homebrew-provided LLVM/Clang
  toolchain (e.g. `llvm@16`) as the frontend compiler.

  \section install_macos_ar Apple ar/ranlib silently drop non-Mach-O archive members

  \subsection install_macos_ar_symptom Symptom
  A native macOS build completes without any configure or compile errors, but `bambu` later fails during HLS synthesis
  with errors such as:
  \verbatim
  error -> The library miss this function __float_mul (called_function)
  \endverbatim
  or similar "library miss this function" errors for other runtime helpers (soft-float, libm, pthread, ...), even
  though the corresponding source file (e.g. `etc/libbambu/softfloat/softfloat.c`) is present and was compiled
  without error.

  \subsection install_macos_ar_cause Cause
  The `etc/libbambu` resource libraries (`libbambu`, `libm`, `libsoftfloat`, `soft-fp`, `pthread`, ...) are not meant
  to be linked by a native linker. Their `.o` members are produced by `tree-panda-gcc` as gzip-compressed LLVM
  bitcode wrapped to look like object files, and are parsed directly by Bambu itself at HLS time - they are never
  valid Mach-O objects.

  The `ar`/`ranlib` shipped with Xcode Command Line Tools (`/usr/bin/ar`, `/usr/bin/ranlib`) silently drop any
  archive member they do not recognize as Mach-O when the archive's symbol table is (re)built, instead of storing it
  as an opaque member the way GNU/LLVM `ar` does. The result is a `.a` file that contains only the `__.SYMDEF SORTED`
  symbol table and none of the actual objects - i.e. an effectively empty runtime library - which is why Bambu can't
  find e.g. `__float_mul` at synthesis time.

  You can confirm this is happening by inspecting one of the built archives, e.g.:
  \verbatim
  ar t <prefix>/lib/panda/libsoftfloat_clang16.a
  \endverbatim
  If this prints only `__.SYMDEF SORTED` (no `.o` member listed), the archive is empty. During the build you will
  also see warnings such as:
  \verbatim
  ranlib: warning: archive member 'libsoftfloat_clang16_a-softfloat.o' not a mach-o file
  \endverbatim
  These warnings are easy to miss/ignore in the middle of a long build log, but they are the actual root cause.

  \subsection install_macos_ar_fix Fix
  Use LLVM's `ar`/`ranlib` instead of Apple's. They ship alongside the Clang/LLVM toolchain (e.g. via
  `brew install llvm@16`, under `$(brew --prefix llvm@16)/bin/llvm-ar` and `llvm-ranlib`) and store any file as an
  archive member regardless of its format, matching the behavior PandA's build expects on Linux.

  Most of the `etc/libbambu` archives are built through Automake's standard archiving rules, which honor `$(AR)`/
  `$(RANLIB)` and so can simply be overridden on the `make` command line:
  \verbatim
  make AR=llvm-ar RANLIB=llvm-ranlib
  make AR=llvm-ar RANLIB=llvm-ranlib install
  \endverbatim

  `libbambu_clang16.a` is the one exception: it is produced by `tree-panda-gcc`'s own archive-merging code path
  (see `command = "ar cru " + ...` in `src/tree-panda-gcc.cpp`), which shells out to a hardcoded `ar` rather than
  respecting `$(AR)`. Overriding `AR=` on the `make` command line has no effect on this step. To fix it too, make
  sure `ar`/`ranlib` resolve to the LLVM versions via `$PATH` for the duration of the build, for example by placing
  symlinks ahead of `/usr/bin` in `$PATH`:
  \verbatim
  mkdir -p /tmp/llvm-ar-shim
  ln -sf "$(brew --prefix llvm@16)/bin/llvm-ar"     /tmp/llvm-ar-shim/ar
  ln -sf "$(brew --prefix llvm@16)/bin/llvm-ranlib" /tmp/llvm-ar-shim/ranlib
  PATH="/tmp/llvm-ar-shim:$PATH" make AR=llvm-ar RANLIB=llvm-ranlib
  PATH="/tmp/llvm-ar-shim:$PATH" make AR=llvm-ar RANLIB=llvm-ranlib install
  \endverbatim

  If you already ran a build/install before applying this workaround, the stale empty archives under your build
  directory and install prefix will not be regenerated just by re-running `make` (the `.a` target is already
  up to date as far as `make` is concerned). Delete them first so the archiving rules re-run:
  \verbatim
  find <builddir>/etc/libbambu -name '*.a' -delete
  \endverbatim
  then rebuild/install as shown above. Verify the fix with the same `ar t` check from
  \ref install_macos_ar_symptom "the symptom section" above - you should now see the real `.o` member(s) listed.

*/
